<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Teniente</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Teniente is the largest underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine in the world, in the Andes near Rancagua in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. It was developed from 1905 by the Braden Copper Company, later owned by Kennecott, and supported the mining town of Sewell. Nationalised with the other large copper mines in 1971, it is now operated by the state company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codelco">Codelco</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-teniente.jpg" alt="El Teniente" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Schubert · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fundici%C3%B3n_de_cobre_en_El_Teniente.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The mine lies at an elevation of 2,300 metres in the Andes. Its ore is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> porphyry deposit that forms a continuous ring about 610 metres wide around the Braden Pipe, a geological structure shaped like an inverted cone with a surface diameter of 1,200 metres. Copper mineralization and the formation of the pipe took place in the Pliocene, helped by the site's position where two large fault systems meet. Ore was first worked in the Fortuna orebody and, from 1922, also in the larger Teniente orebody on the east side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By legend a fugitive Spanish official discovered the deposit. Mining began in 1819, the best ore being dug by hand and carried out on ponies and mules until the high-grade ore ran out in 1897. In 1904 William Braden, an engineer from New York City, and E. W. Nash formed the Braden Copper Company, which built a cart road and a concentrating plant. Guggenheim Exploration took control in June 1910, and in 1916 Braden became a subsidiary of the Kennecott Copper Corporation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>.</p><p>In 1945 a fire in a warehouse filled the tunnels with smoke while some 1,000 miners were underground; 355 men died and 747 were injured, most from carbon monoxide poisoning. Known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> as the Smoke Tragedy, it was the deadliest metal mining accident in the world. The Chilean government bought a 51% stake in 1967, and on 11 July 1971 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a> nationalized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> industry; Chile paid Kennecott $92.9 million for the property. In 1983 the mine closed when workers struck over the arrest of a union leader who had called for an end to military rule, and at least 3,300 strikers in three mines were dismissed.</p><p>On 31 July 2025 a magnitude 4.2 earthquake caused a collapse in the mine. More than 100 rescuers took part in the search, and by 2 August 2025 six workers had been found dead; the regional prosecutor then opened a criminal investigation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>El Teniente is the largest division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codelco">Codelco</a>. It has more than 3,000 kilometres of tunnels and about 5,000 workers, and in 2011 work began on the New Mine Level project, designed to extend the mine deeper into the mountain without halting production. Its tailings are also treated for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and molybdenum concentrate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El Teniente” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Copper, mined largely by American companies at <b>El Teniente</b> and Chuquicamata, then became the main export; it was nationalised in 1971 and later placed under the state company Codelco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
